y, conversational tone and address the "common knowledge" idea head-on. It's one of those things "everybody knows" that is, in fact, not true.
14:26:35 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is absolutely one of the most widespread urban legends out there! You are definitely not alone in thinking that—almost everyone grows up believing they are weight sensors, and it is frequently repeated as "common knowledge." The reason this myth is so believable is because it makes perfect intuitive sense. We are used to things working that way in everyday life. For example, the automatic doors at older grocery stores used to open when you stepped on a pressure-sensitive mat. It feels perfectly logical that a car pulling up to a red light would press down on a giant button in the road to turn it green. But from an engineering standpoint, putting mechanical weight sensors in a public road would be a massive nightmare. Here is why civil engineers don't use them: **1. The Extreme Weight Differences** A sensor would have to be sensitive enough to detect a 400-pound motorcycle, but strong enough to survive a fully loaded, 80,000-pound 18-wheeler slamming on its brakes directly on top of it. A mechanical scale or pressure plate simply cannot survive that kind of repeated abuse. **2. Weather and Moving Parts** Weight sensors require moving parts (like springs or pressure plates) to register that something is pushing down on them. If you put moving parts in a road, rain will get inside. In the winter, that water will freeze into ice, expanding and jamming the sensor so it can't move. **3. Snowplows** If a pressure plate sat even slightly above the road surface, the steel blade of a snowplow would catch it and rip it completely out of the asphalt. **4. Constant Maintenance** Because mechanical sensors would break constantly from heavy trucks and weather, city workers would have to shut down intersections and tear up the asphalt every few weeks just to replace them. **The Illusion** Because the magnetic **inductive loop detectors** (the wire coils I mentioned earlier) are buried exactly where your tires stop, and the light changes shortly after you roll to a halt, your brain naturally connects the *weight* of your car stopping to the light changing. It is a perfect illusion! So, while it is definitely "common knowledge," it is one of those rare cases where the common knowledge is actually a very well-disguised myth. Engineers use magnets, radar, and cameras specifically because they have **no moving parts** and can survive the harsh environment of a city street.
