14:29:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I completely get why you'd say that. When you phrase it as an "invisible magnetic force field," it sounds like some ridiculously expensive, over-engineered technology out of *Star Trek*! It seems totally backwards that a city would choose sci-fi technology over a simple, physical button. But here is the wild part: to a civil engineer, the "magnetic force field" is actually the **simplest, cheapest, and lowest-tech option possible.** Here is why our everyday definition of "simple" is the exact opposite of an engineer's definition of "simple": ### The Problem with "Simple" Weight Sensors Think about a simple weight sensor, like your bathroom scale or the button on a keyboard. For it to work, it requires **moving parts**. Something has to physically press down, compress a spring or a hinge, make an electrical contact, and then bounce back up. Now, imagine taking your bathroom scale, burying it in the asphalt, and subjecting it to this: * An 80,000-pound semi-truck slams its brakes directly on top of it. * A torrential rainstorm floods it with water. * Winter hits, and that water freezes into solid ice, expanding and jamming the springs. * A snowplow scrapes a heavy steel blade directly over the top of it. * Sand, gravel, and motor oil seep into the cracks. To make a mechanical weight sensor survive that kind of brutal punishment, it couldn't be simple. It would have to be a massive, incredibly complex, heavy-duty industrial scale with reinforced steel, waterproof gaskets, and heavy-duty hydraulics. It would cost a fortune to build and install at every single lane of every single intersection. ### The "Sci-Fi" Magnet is Actually Just a Wire When I say "electromagnetic field," it sounds highly advanced. But an inductive loop detector is literally just **a cheap copper wire wrapped in a circle.** That’s it. If you take a regular piece of copper wire, wrap it in a loop, and plug it into a battery, you have just created an electromagnetic field. It is 19th-century technology. You could build one in your garage for a few dollars. Because it is literally just a wire, it has **zero moving parts**. * It doesn't need to be pressed down. * It doesn't have hinges or springs that can freeze or get jammed with dirt. * A city worker just takes a circular saw, cuts a shallow groove in the asphalt, drops the wire inside, and pours hot tar over it. Once that wire is sealed inside the tar, it is completely protected from trucks, rain, ice, and snowplows. It can sit there for 20 years without anyone ever needing to fix it. ### Cities are Cheap! At the end of the day, city governments don't want to spend money if they don't have to. If they used mechanical weight plates, they would have to send road crews out constantly to dig up the street and fix jammed springs or broken sensors. By using a simple copper wire buried in tar, the only complex part (the computer) sits safely inside a metal box on the sidewalk. If something breaks, the technician just opens the box on the sidewalk—they don't have to shut down traffic to dig up the street. So, while it sounds totally ridiculous and over-complicated to use invisible magnetic fields, it turns out that a piece of wire buried in tar is actually the cheapest, laziest, and most durable solution humans have ever invented!
